  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 1124 PM CST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nTuesday Night-Wednesday, a strong shortwave to track over the Plains \ninto IA/MO. There remains some subtle differences in the track of \nthis wave with the 12z ECMWF/18z RRFS further south than the 18z \nGFS. This results in continued low confidence in the location of \nPoPs/rainfall due to available moisture. If the southern solution \nverifies than areas along and south of I-80 would see the best \nchances for rain, albeit light amounts. There could even be a \nscenario where much of the area remains dry except our far \nsouthwest over northeast MO and the far north along Hwy 20. \nRegardless, the wave will be progressive and rain amounts light,\nwith NBM 24-hr probabilities for QPF > 0.1\" only in the 15-35% \nrange. Otherwise, a mostly cloudy day can be expected with high \ntemperatures topping out in the middle 50s. \n\nWednesday Night-Sunday, another strong shortwave to drop south in \nthe northwest flow aloft on Thursday. Both the deterministic and \nensemble runs of the GFS are faster and wetter with this system \ncompared to the ECMWF solutions. As a result, the NBM has slight \nchance (<20%) PoPs across northwest and north central IL. What is of \nhigher confidence, is the colder air arriving behind this wave. High \ntemperatures to drop closer if not a few degrees colder than normal \ninto the low 40s Thursday and Friday, especially if clouds stick \naround longer. After Friday, a large area of high pressure and a \nbuilding upper ridge over the central CONUS to bring another period \nof dry conditions and above normal temperatures.",
